-- Q1: medium / L2 — Interest income sourcing rule (residence of payer)
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Harmon Industries, a U.S. corporation, receives $400,000 in interest income from a loan it made to Centrex Ltd., a corporation incorporated and operating exclusively in Germany. Under IRC Sections 861-862, how is this interest income sourced for U.S. federal income tax purposes?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Foreign-source income because interest is sourced to the residence of the payer", "U.S.-source income because the lender is a domestic corporation", "Foreign-source income because the loan proceeds were used abroad", "U.S.-source income because the loan agreement was executed in the U.S."]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 861(a)(1) and Sec. 862(a)(1), interest income is generally sourced based on the residence (country of incorporation) of the payer. Centrex Ltd. is a foreign corporation, so the interest is foreign-source income. Wrong (B): The lender's residence determines the sourcing of interest it pays, not interest it receives. Wrong (C): The use of loan proceeds does not determine interest sourcing under the general rule. Wrong (D): The location of contract execution is irrelevant to interest income sourcing.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Branch vs subsidiary: current taxation vs deferral
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Orion Corp, a U.S. parent, is deciding between establishing a foreign branch or a foreign subsidiary in Country K. The Country K operation is expected to generate $1,200,000 of operating income in its first year. Evaluate which structure results in current U.S. taxation of the Country K income.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Both structures result in identical timing of U.S. taxation", "A foreign branch results in current U.S. taxation because branch income flows through to the U.S. parent, while a foreign subsidiary's income is generally deferred", "A foreign subsidiary results in current taxation because subsidiary income must be reported immediately", "Neither structure results in current U.S. taxation because foreign income is exempt"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under U.S. tax law, a foreign branch is not a separate taxable entity; its income flows through to the U.S. parent and is taxed currently. A foreign subsidiary is a separate legal entity whose income is generally deferred from U.S. taxation until distributed or captured by anti-deferral rules. Wrong (A): The two structures have fundamentally different tax timing. Wrong (C): This reverses the treatment; subsidiary income is generally deferred. Wrong (D): The U.S. taxes worldwide income of U.S. persons; foreign income is not exempt.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Worldwide taxation principle
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Under the U.S. federal income tax system, on what basis are U.S. citizens and resident aliens generally taxed?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Only on income earned within the United States", "Only on income received in or remitted to the United States", "On their worldwide income, regardless of where it is earned", "Only on income from countries that have a tax agreement with the U.S."]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 61, the U.S. taxes its citizens and resident aliens on worldwide income regardless of where it is earned. This worldwide system is a foundational principle of U.S. international taxation. Wrong (A): This describes a territorial system, which the U.S. does not use for individuals. Wrong (B): A remittance basis applies in some other countries but not the U.S. Wrong (D): The scope of U.S. taxing jurisdiction over its citizens is not limited by bilateral agreements.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: easy / L2 — Dividend income sourcing (country of incorporation of payer)
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Waverly Inc., a U.S. corporation, receives a $200,000 dividend from Delphi SA, a corporation incorporated in France that earns all of its income from operations in Brazil. Under the income sourcing rules, how is the dividend income classified?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["U.S.-source because Waverly is a domestic corporation", "Brazilian-source because Delphi earns its income in Brazil", "Sourced to the country where the dividend payment is deposited", "Foreign-source because dividends are sourced to the payer's country of incorporation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 862(a)(2), dividends from a foreign corporation are generally foreign-source income. The sourcing follows the residence (country of incorporation) of the payer, which is France. Wrong (A): The recipient's residence does not determine dividend sourcing. Wrong (B): Where the payer earns its income does not control dividend sourcing under the general rule. Wrong (C): The deposit location of funds is irrelevant to income sourcing.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Personal services income sourcing (where performed)
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Nakamura, a U.S. citizen and independent consultant, performs management consulting services in Tokyo for a Japanese client over a three-month engagement, earning $150,000. The client pays by wire to Nakamura's U.S. bank account. How is this consulting income sourced?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Foreign-source because personal services income is sourced where the services are performed", "U.S.-source because payment was received in the United States", "U.S.-source because Nakamura is a U.S. citizen", "Split between U.S. and foreign sources based on the client's nationality"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 861(a)(3) and Sec. 862(a)(3), compensation for personal services is sourced to the place where the services are performed. Because Nakamura performed the work in Japan, the income is foreign-source regardless of payment location. Wrong (B): The location of payment receipt does not determine personal services income sourcing. Wrong (C): Citizenship determines worldwide tax obligation but does not change source classification. Wrong (D): The client's nationality is irrelevant to personal services income sourcing.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Withholding agent obligation and 30% statutory rate
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Pinnacle Corp, a U.S. corporation, pays $500,000 in royalties to Solaris GmbH, a German corporation with no U.S. operations. Pinnacle's controller asks whether Pinnacle must withhold U.S. tax on this payment and, if so, what the default statutory rate is. Analyze Pinnacle's withholding responsibility.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Pinnacle has no withholding obligation because Solaris is a foreign corporation", "Pinnacle must withhold at the statutory rate of 30% on U.S.-source FDAP income paid to a foreign person, though bilateral agreements may reduce the rate", "Pinnacle must withhold at 15% because royalties are classified as investment income", "Pinnacle's obligation depends solely on whether Solaris files a U.S. tax return"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 1441, a U.S. person paying U.S.-source FDAP income (including royalties) to a foreign person must withhold at the statutory rate of 30%. Bilateral tax agreements between the U.S. and the payee's country may reduce this rate. Pinnacle is the withholding agent. Wrong (A): U.S. payors have a statutory obligation to withhold on U.S.-source FDAP income paid to foreign persons. Wrong (C): The 15% rate is not a statutory default; the default is 30%. Wrong (D): The withholding obligation exists regardless of whether the foreign payee files a U.S. return.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Branch income: regular tax and current inclusion analysis
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Aldrich Manufacturing, a U.S. corporation, operates a factory branch in Country M that generates $3,000,000 of operating income. Aldrich also owns 100% of Vantage Ltd., a foreign subsidiary in Country N that earns $2,000,000. Evaluate how each structure affects the timing of Aldrich's current-year U.S. taxable income.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Both the branch and subsidiary income are deferred from U.S. taxation until repatriation", "The subsidiary income is currently taxable while the branch income is deferred", "The branch income of $3,000,000 is included currently because branch income flows through, while subsidiary income is generally deferred until distributed or captured by anti-deferral provisions", "Both are included currently because the U.S. taxes worldwide income without deferral"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): A foreign branch is not a separate entity for U.S. tax purposes, so its $3,000,000 flows through to Aldrich's U.S. return currently. A foreign subsidiary is a separate legal entity whose income is generally deferred until repatriated or captured by anti-deferral rules. Wrong (A): Branch income is not eligible for deferral; it flows through currently. Wrong (B): This reverses the treatment of the two structures. Wrong (D): Subsidiary income benefits from deferral under the general rule.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: hard / L4 — PE analysis: dependent agent vs independent agent
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Kyoto Electronics, a Japanese corporation, sells consumer electronics in the U.S. through two arrangements: (1) Fulton Sales LLC, an independent distributor that buys and resells at arm's length, and (2) Tanaka, a U.S.-based employee who negotiates and concludes contracts on Kyoto's behalf. Kyoto has no U.S. office. Evaluate whether Kyoto has a permanent establishment.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No PE exists because Kyoto has no fixed office or warehouse in the U.S.", "No PE exists because all U.S. sales are conducted through third parties", "No PE exists because foreign manufacturers are exempt from PE rules", "Kyoto likely has a PE because Tanaka is a dependent agent with authority to conclude contracts on its behalf"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): A PE can arise through a dependent agent who habitually exercises authority to conclude contracts on behalf of the foreign enterprise. Tanaka meets this criterion. Fulton is an independent agent acting in its own business and does not create a PE. Wrong (A): A fixed office is not required; a dependent agent with contracting authority can constitute a PE. Wrong (B): While Fulton is independent, Tanaka is a dependent agent whose activities create PE exposure. Wrong (C): No blanket exemption from PE rules exists for foreign manufacturers.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Rental income sourcing (location of property)
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Granite Holdings, a U.S. corporation, owns a commercial building in London leased to British tenants for $800,000 annually and a warehouse in Chicago leased to a British company for $300,000 annually. Analyze the sourcing of each rental income stream.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["London rental is foreign-source and Chicago rental is U.S.-source because rental income is sourced to the property location", "Both are U.S.-source because Granite is a U.S. corporation", "Both are foreign-source because the tenants include foreign persons", "London rental is U.S.-source and Chicago rental is foreign-source"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 861(a)(4) and Sec. 862(a)(4), rental income is sourced to the location of the property. The London building produces foreign-source income; the Chicago warehouse produces U.S.-source income. The nationality of landlord or tenant is irrelevant. Wrong (B): The landlord's country of incorporation does not control rental income sourcing. Wrong (C): The tenant's nationality does not determine rental income sourcing. Wrong (D): This reverses the correct rule; sourcing follows the property location.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: hard / L3 — ECI concept: foreign corp with U.S. trade or business
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Bergstrom AB, a Swedish corporation, opens a retail store in New York City and earns $2,500,000 in sales revenue from U.S. customers. Bergstrom has no other U.S. activities. Analyze the basis on which Bergstrom is subject to U.S. federal income tax on this revenue.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Bergstrom is subject to U.S. tax solely because it receives payments from U.S. customers", "Bergstrom is taxed on its effectively connected income because operating a retail store constitutes a U.S. trade or business", "Bergstrom is exempt from U.S. tax because it is a foreign corporation", "Bergstrom owes U.S. tax only on revenue that exceeds its cost of goods sold"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 882, a foreign corporation engaged in a U.S. trade or business is taxed on its effectively connected income (ECI). Operating a retail store constitutes a trade or business, and the sales revenue is ECI subject to U.S. corporate tax rates. Wrong (A): Receiving payments from U.S. customers alone does not create U.S. tax liability without a U.S. trade or business. Wrong (C): Foreign corporations are subject to U.S. tax on ECI and certain U.S.-source income. Wrong (D): ECI is subject to tax with deductions under normal rules, not a simple revenue-minus-COGS test.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q11: hard / L4 — Sale of inventory sourcing: title passage and production-sale split
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Pacific Rim Foods, a U.S. corporation, manufactures food products entirely in Oregon and sells them to unrelated distributors in Country J. Title passes at the port in Country J. Pacific Rim earned $5,000,000. Evaluate the income sourcing under the production-and-sale rules.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["All $5,000,000 is U.S.-source because the goods were manufactured in the U.S.", "All $5,000,000 is foreign-source because title passed outside the U.S.", "Income must be allocated between U.S. and foreign sources because production and sale occurred in different countries", "Sourcing is based solely on the buyer's country of residence"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 863(b), when a taxpayer produces and sells inventory, and production and sale occur in different countries, the income must be apportioned between U.S. and foreign sources. Production in Oregon creates a U.S.-source component; title passage in Country J creates a foreign-source component. Wrong (A): Production location alone does not control sourcing when the sale occurs in a different jurisdiction. Wrong (B): Title passage alone does not control when production occurs elsewhere. Wrong (D): The buyer's residence is not a sourcing factor for inventory sales under these rules.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — CFC definition: 10% U.S. shareholder threshold
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$What ownership percentage must a U.S. person hold in a foreign corporation to qualify as a "U.S. shareholder" for controlled foreign corporation purposes?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["More than 50% of the total voting power", "More than 25% of the total value of stock", "5% of the total combined voting power or value", "At least 10% of the total combined voting power or value of stock"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 951(b), a U.S. shareholder is any U.S. person who owns at least 10% of the total combined voting power or value of a foreign corporation's stock. This threshold determines which shareholders count toward the CFC test. Wrong (A): The more-than-50% threshold applies to CFC status, not individual shareholder classification. Wrong (B): 25% is not a threshold used in the CFC ownership rules. Wrong (C): 5% is not the applicable threshold; it is 10%.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Sale of real property sourcing (location of property)
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Westgate Corp, a U.S. real estate company, sells a commercial building in Toronto, Canada, to an unrelated Canadian buyer for $4,000,000, realizing a $1,200,000 gain. The sale is negotiated and closed at Westgate's Dallas headquarters. How is the gain sourced?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Foreign-source because gain from selling real property is sourced to the property location", "U.S.-source because the seller is a U.S. corporation", "U.S.-source because the transaction was negotiated and closed in the U.S.", "Split between U.S. and foreign sources based on buyer and seller nationalities"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 861(a)(5) and Sec. 862(a)(5), gains from the sale of real property are sourced to the country where the property is located. The Toronto building produces foreign-source gain. Wrong (B): The seller's country of incorporation does not control real property gain sourcing. Wrong (C): The location of negotiations is irrelevant to real property gain sourcing. Wrong (D): Buyer and seller nationalities do not factor into sourcing real property gains.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Distinguish income sourcing from income taxability
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Rivera, a tax associate, states in a memo that because certain income of a U.S. corporation is classified as foreign-source under the sourcing rules, the corporation does not owe U.S. tax on that income. The senior reviewer identifies an error. Evaluate the flaw in Rivera's reasoning.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Rivera is correct because foreign-source income is never subject to U.S. taxation", "The flaw is that income sourcing determines where income is deemed earned, not whether it is taxable; U.S. corporations are taxed on worldwide income regardless of source", "The flaw is that foreign-source income is taxed at a higher rate than U.S.-source income", "Rivera is correct for corporations but wrong for individuals"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Income sourcing and income taxability are related but distinct concepts. Under IRC Sec. 11 and Sec. 61, a U.S. corporation is taxed on worldwide income. Source classification matters for certain provisions but does not exempt income from U.S. tax. Wrong (A): The U.S. taxes domestic corporations on worldwide income, including foreign-source income. Wrong (C): There is no higher rate for foreign-source income; the same corporate rate applies. Wrong (D): Both U.S. corporations and U.S. individuals are taxed on worldwide income.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — PE definition: fixed place of business
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$What generally constitutes a permanent establishment for a foreign corporation operating in the United States?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Any sales transaction completed with a U.S. customer", "Any employee who travels to the United States on business", "A fixed place of business through which the enterprise carries on its business activities", "Registration of a corporate name with a U.S. state agency"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): A permanent establishment is generally defined as a fixed place of business through which the enterprise wholly or partly carries on its business. This includes offices, factories, workshops, and similar locations. Wrong (A): A single sales transaction does not create a PE; a fixed and ongoing presence is required. Wrong (B): Employee travel alone does not establish a PE without a fixed place of business or dependent agent authority. Wrong (D): Mere corporate name registration does not constitute a fixed place of business.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: hard / L4 — Multi-income sourcing analysis across income types
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$GlobalTech Corp, a U.S. corporation, earns: (1) $600,000 interest from a loan to Nexon Ltd., incorporated in South Korea; (2) $400,000 from consulting services performed in Germany; (3) $300,000 rental income from a Chicago office building leased to a foreign tenant. Determine GlobalTech's total foreign-source income.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$400,000 — only the consulting income is foreign-source", "$1,300,000 — all income involving a foreign element is foreign-source", "$600,000 — only interest from a foreign payer qualifies", "$1,000,000 — the interest and consulting income are foreign-source; the Chicago rental is U.S.-source"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 862(a)(1), interest is sourced to the payer's residence (South Korea, $600,000 foreign-source). Under Sec. 862(a)(3), services income is sourced where performed (Germany, $400,000 foreign-source). Under Sec. 861(a)(4), rental income is sourced to the property location (Chicago, U.S.-source). Total foreign-source: $1,000,000. Wrong (A): This omits the foreign-source interest income. Wrong (B): The Chicago rental is U.S.-source regardless of the tenant's nationality. Wrong (C): This omits the foreign-source consulting income.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Withholding: FDAP income vs goods purchase
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Sterling Corp, a U.S. corporation, makes two payments to unrelated foreign corporations with no U.S. business: (1) $200,000 in interest on a loan; (2) $150,000 for raw materials purchased and shipped from abroad. Which payment is subject to the 30% statutory withholding?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The interest payment, because interest is FDAP income subject to withholding under IRC Sec. 1441", "The raw materials payment, because any payment to a foreign person requires withholding", "Both payments require withholding at the 30% statutory rate", "Neither payment requires withholding because the payees are corporations"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 1441, U.S.-source FDAP income (including interest, dividends, rents, and royalties) paid to foreign persons is subject to 30% statutory withholding. Interest qualifies as FDAP income. Payments for goods are not FDAP and are not subject to withholding. Wrong (B): Payments for goods purchased are not FDAP income and do not trigger withholding. Wrong (C): Only FDAP payments are subject to withholding, not goods purchases. Wrong (D): Corporate status of the payee does not exempt FDAP payments from withholding.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Bilateral agreement withholding rate reduction concept
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Apex Corp, a U.S. corporation, pays $1,000,000 in dividends to its sole shareholder, Bergen Holdings, a corporation incorporated in a country that has a bilateral tax agreement with the United States. Apex withholds 30% ($300,000). Bergen submits proper documentation claiming a reduced withholding rate. Analyze whether Apex withheld correctly.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Apex withheld correctly because 30% is always the final rate on dividends to foreign shareholders", "Apex may have overwithheld because bilateral tax agreements commonly reduce the statutory 30% withholding rate, and proper documentation may entitle Bergen to a lower rate", "Apex underwithheld because dividends to foreign shareholders are subject to 35%", "The withholding rate is irrelevant because Bergen can claim a full refund regardless"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): The 30% rate under IRC Sec. 1441 is the statutory default. Bilateral tax agreements between the U.S. and other countries frequently reduce withholding on dividends, interest, and royalties. If Bergen qualifies for a reduced rate and submits proper documentation, Apex should withhold at that lower rate. Wrong (A): The 30% rate is the default; bilateral agreements may reduce it. Wrong (C): The statutory withholding rate is 30%, not 35%. Wrong (D): Overwithholding creates a real cash flow burden; the correct rate should be applied at source.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: easy / L2 — Foreign subsidiary deferral concept
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Colton Inc., a U.S. corporation, owns 100% of Vistara Ltd., a foreign subsidiary incorporated in Singapore. Vistara earns $3,000,000 in operating income but pays no dividends during the year. How is Vistara's income generally treated on Colton's U.S. return?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Colton must include Vistara's full $3,000,000 on its return immediately", "Colton reports 50% of the subsidiary's income currently", "Generally not included until distributed as a dividend or captured by anti-deferral provisions", "Vistara's income is permanently exempt from U.S. taxation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the general rule, a foreign subsidiary is a separate legal entity. Its income is not included in the U.S. parent's taxable income until repatriated as a dividend or captured by anti-deferral rules. This contrasts with a foreign branch. Wrong (A): Subsidiary income is generally deferred, not immediately included, absent anti-deferral provisions. Wrong (B): There is no 50% inclusion rule for subsidiary income. Wrong (D): The income is not permanently exempt; it is subject to U.S. tax upon distribution.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q20: easy / L2 — Interest paid by U.S. corporation is generally U.S.-source
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Beacon Corp, a U.S. corporation, borrows $10,000,000 from a Swiss bank and pays $500,000 in annual interest. The loan funds are used entirely in Germany. From the Swiss bank's perspective, how is the interest income classified under U.S. sourcing rules?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Foreign-source because the loan funds were used in Germany", "Foreign-source because the Swiss bank is a foreign lender", "Split equally between U.S. and foreign sources", "U.S.-source because interest is sourced to the residence of the payer"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 861(a)(1), interest paid by a domestic corporation is generally U.S.-source income. The residence of the payer controls the sourcing, regardless of where borrowed funds are deployed. Wrong (A): The use of loan proceeds does not determine interest income sourcing under the general rule. Wrong (B): The lender's residence does not determine the source of interest it receives. Wrong (C): There is no equal-split rule for interest income sourcing.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Purpose of credit for foreign taxes paid
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$What is the primary purpose of the U.S. credit for income taxes paid to foreign governments?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["To mitigate double taxation by crediting foreign income taxes against U.S. tax liability", "To exempt all foreign-source income from U.S. taxation", "To increase the effective tax rate on foreign operations", "To penalize U.S. corporations that earn income abroad"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 901, U.S. taxpayers may claim a credit for foreign income taxes paid against their U.S. tax liability. Because the U.S. taxes worldwide income, this credit mitigates double taxation when the same income is taxed by both a foreign country and the United States. Wrong (B): The credit does not exempt foreign income; it reduces the U.S. tax burden on that income. Wrong (C): The credit reduces, not increases, the effective tax on foreign income. Wrong (D): The credit is a relief mechanism, not a penalty.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — CFC: more than 50% collective ownership test
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$What collective ownership threshold must U.S. shareholders meet for a foreign corporation to be classified as a controlled foreign corporation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Exactly 50% of total combined voting power or value", "More than 50% of total combined voting power or value of stock", "At least 25% of total combined voting power", "Any level of U.S. ownership creates CFC status"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 957(a), a CFC is a foreign corporation in which U.S. shareholders (each owning at least 10%) collectively own more than 50% of the total combined voting power or value. The threshold is strictly more than 50%. Wrong (A): Exactly 50% does not meet the threshold; it must exceed 50%. Wrong (C): The threshold is more than 50%, not 25%, and includes value as well as voting power. Wrong (D): CFC status requires the more-than-50% threshold to be met by qualifying U.S. shareholders.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: medium / L2 — Sale of personal property (non-inventory): seller's residence
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Kirkwood Corp, a U.S. corporation, sells equipment used in its domestic operations to an unrelated buyer in Australia for a $350,000 gain. The equipment was in Kirkwood's U.S. factory at the time of sale. How is the gain sourced under the general rule for non-inventory personal property?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Foreign-source because the buyer is located in Australia", "U.S.-source because the equipment was physically in the United States", "U.S.-source because gain on non-inventory personal property is generally sourced to the seller's residence", "Split between U.S. and foreign sources based on negotiation location"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 865(a), gain from the sale of personal property (other than inventory) is generally sourced based on the seller's residence. Kirkwood is a U.S. corporation, so the gain is U.S.-source. Wrong (A): The buyer's location does not determine sourcing for non-inventory personal property. Wrong (B): While the gain is U.S.-source, the correct rule is seller's residence, not the property's physical location. Wrong (D): Negotiation location is not a sourcing factor for personal property gains.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Foreign branch: income flows through to U.S. parent
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$How is the income of a foreign branch of a U.S. corporation treated for U.S. federal income tax purposes?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Deferred until the branch remits profits to the U.S. parent", "Permanently exempt from U.S. taxation because it is earned abroad", "Taxed only by the host country under a territorial exemption", "Included in the U.S. parent's taxable income currently because a branch is not a separate entity"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): A foreign branch is not a separate legal entity from its U.S. parent for federal income tax purposes. The branch's income and expenses flow through directly to the U.S. corporation's tax return and are taxed currently. Wrong (A): Deferral applies to foreign subsidiaries, not branches. Wrong (B): Branch income is not exempt; the U.S. taxes it currently as worldwide income. Wrong (C): The U.S. does not provide a territorial exemption for branch income.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Constructive ownership and CFC determination
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Whitmore, a U.S. citizen, directly owns 8% of ForCo, a foreign corporation. Whitmore's wholly owned U.S. corporation separately owns 5% of ForCo. Under the constructive ownership rules, what is Whitmore's total ownership for U.S. shareholder determination?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["13%, combining direct 8% with attributed 5%, making Whitmore a U.S. shareholder", "8%, because only direct ownership counts for the determination", "5%, because only the corporate ownership is attributed", "0%, because Whitmore's direct stake is below 10%"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 958(b), which incorporates the constructive ownership rules of Sec. 318(a), stock owned by a corporation is attributed to its shareholders proportionally. Whitmore owns 100% of the corporation, so its 5% in ForCo is attributed to him. Combined with his direct 8%, Whitmore constructively owns 13%, exceeding the 10% threshold. Wrong (B): Constructive ownership rules apply; direct ownership alone is not the complete test. Wrong (C): Both direct and constructive ownership are combined. Wrong (D): Constructive ownership applies regardless of whether the direct stake alone meets the threshold.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — ECI vs FDAP distinction for foreign corporations
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Solberg AS, a Norwegian corporation, earns $1,500,000 from operating a consulting office in Boston and $200,000 in interest from a U.S. bank deposit. Solberg has no other U.S. activities. How are these two income streams taxed differently?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Both are taxed identically at a flat 30% withholding rate", "The consulting income is ECI taxed at regular corporate rates on a net basis, while the bank interest is FDAP income subject to 30% gross-basis withholding", "The bank interest is taxed at regular rates while the consulting income is subject to 30% withholding", "Neither is subject to U.S. tax because Solberg is a foreign corporation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 882, a foreign corporation's ECI from a U.S. trade or business is taxed at regular corporate rates on a net-income basis. Under IRC Sec. 881, FDAP income not effectively connected with a U.S. business is generally subject to 30% withholding on the gross amount. Wrong (A): ECI and FDAP income are taxed under different regimes with different rate structures. Wrong (C): This reverses the correct treatment of ECI and FDAP income. Wrong (D): Foreign corporations are subject to U.S. tax on both ECI and U.S.-source FDAP income.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — PE: preparatory/auxiliary activities exclusion
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Zurich Pharma AG, a Swiss corporation, maintains a small San Francisco office whose sole function is collecting industry news and forwarding market research to headquarters. No sales, negotiations, or contracts occur there. Analyze whether this office creates a U.S. permanent establishment.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A PE exists because any fixed U.S. office automatically constitutes a PE", "A PE exists because the office employs U.S.-based staff", "A PE likely does not exist because the activities are preparatory and auxiliary in nature", "A PE cannot exist for a pharmaceutical company"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): A fixed place of business used solely for activities of a preparatory or auxiliary character (such as information gathering or market research) is generally excluded from PE status. The San Francisco office performs only these preparatory functions. Wrong (A): Not every fixed office constitutes a PE; the nature of activities determines PE status. Wrong (B): Employing local staff does not automatically create a PE if activities are preparatory or auxiliary. Wrong (D): No industry-specific PE exemption exists for pharmaceutical companies.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: hard / L4 — Foreign corp: multiple income types, sourcing and taxability
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Yokohama Corp, a Japanese corporation with no U.S. office or employees, earns: (1) $800,000 from selling goods to U.S. customers with title passing in Japan; (2) $200,000 in dividends from a U.S. public company; (3) $150,000 in interest from a U.S. corporate bond. Evaluate which items are subject to U.S. tax.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["All three are taxable because they involve U.S. counterparties", "Only the goods sales income is taxable because it involves U.S. customers", "Only the dividends and interest are taxable as FDAP income", "The dividends and interest are U.S.-source FDAP subject to 30% withholding; the goods sales are likely foreign-source with no ECI"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 861(a)(2), dividends from a U.S. corporation are U.S.-source FDAP. Under Sec. 861(a)(1), interest from a U.S. obligor is U.S.-source FDAP. Both are subject to 30% withholding under Sec. 881. The goods sales, with title passing in Japan and no U.S. trade or business, are likely foreign-source and not ECI. Wrong (A): Having U.S. counterparties does not automatically create U.S. tax liability for foreign corporations. Wrong (B): Goods sales with foreign title passage and no U.S. trade or business are generally not taxable. Wrong (C): While correct on dividends and interest, this does not address the sourcing basis.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Anti-deferral concept: subsidiary income not permanently exempt
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Drake Industries, a U.S. corporation, owns 100% of two foreign subsidiaries. A junior analyst asserts that subsidiary income deferral makes it permanently exempt from U.S. taxation. The senior manager corrects this misunderstanding. Evaluate why the analyst is incorrect.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Deferral is not permanent because subsidiary income becomes taxable when distributed as a dividend, and anti-deferral provisions may require current inclusion of certain income", "Subsidiary income is indeed deferred indefinitely and never subject to U.S. tax", "The analyst is wrong only because subsidiaries must distribute all earnings annually", "The analyst is wrong because all subsidiary income is taxed currently with no deferral"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Foreign subsidiary income deferral is not permanent. Under the general rule, the income becomes subject to U.S. tax when distributed as a dividend to the U.S. parent. Additionally, anti-deferral provisions may require current inclusion of certain types of income even without a distribution. Wrong (B): This incorrectly treats deferral as permanent; it ends upon distribution or under anti-deferral rules. Wrong (C): U.S. law does not require annual distribution of all subsidiary earnings. Wrong (D): Deferral does exist for foreign subsidiaries under the general rule; the error is treating it as permanent.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Construction site PE duration threshold concept
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Hamburg Construction GmbH, a German corporation, wins a contract to build a manufacturing plant in Texas. The project is expected to take 14 months. Hamburg's tax advisor explains that project duration is critical for determining U.S. tax obligations. Analyze why duration matters for PE purposes.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Duration is irrelevant because all construction projects create a PE", "Duration matters because a construction site constitutes a PE only if it exceeds a specified threshold, and 14 months likely exceeds the typical threshold", "Construction projects are permanently exempt from PE rules", "Duration matters only if the project exceeds 36 months"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): A construction site or project generally constitutes a PE only if it lasts more than a specified duration (commonly 12 months). A 14-month project exceeds this typical threshold, meaning the site likely constitutes a PE and Hamburg may owe U.S. tax on the project income. Wrong (A): Not all construction projects create a PE; the duration threshold must be exceeded. Wrong (C): Construction projects are not exempt; they are subject to PE rules with a duration test. Wrong (D): The typical threshold is 12 months, not 36 months.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
